{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>For the wrath has gone forth from before YHWH, the plague has begun.</b> There is an angel whose name is קצף (Wrath), a branch and force drawn from the attribute of judgment, and therefore the plague began. And Moses recognized him from the giving of the Torah, and this is what they expounded in Tanḥuma on the verse (Exodus 32:7) ״go down״ — when they made the calf, five angels of destruction confronted him, and these are they: אף (Anger) and חמה (Wrath) and קצף (Indignation) and השמד (Annihilation) and חרון (Fury). When he mentioned the three patriarchs, three were shaken off from him; אף and חמה remained — this is what is said (Deuteronomy 9:19): ״for I feared because of the anger and the wrath.״ He said to Him: You stand in one, as is said (Psalms 7:7): ״קומה ה' באפך״ — Arise, YHWH, in Your anger; and I in one, as is said (Psalms 106): ״לולי משה בחירו עמד בפרץ לפניו להשיב חמתו מהשחית.״" "<b>And what</b> it says, ״and he stood between the dead and between the living״ — it seems to me that had it said ״between the dead and the living״ it would imply that the dead were in front of him with no living among them, and the living behind him with no dead among them, and Aaron stood in the middle. But now that it says ״between the dead and between the living,״ it indicates that the dead were in front of him and behind him, and likewise the living were in front of him and behind him, and he was in the middle — and this is the sense of ״and he stood between the dead and between the living.״ And this was when he came and offered the incense, for then the plague was halted immediately and Israel remained as he found them: a living person whom he found among the dead did not die, and a dead person who was among the living was not torn away. And we may learn from this that at first, before his arrival, the angel was destroying and smiting everything — putting the living to death and tearing away the dead." "<b>And so</b> you find in Midrash Tanḥuma: What is the meaning of החל (the beginning)? Rabbi Yehuda ben Simon said: The angel was taking in an orderly sequence, and would leave neither a dead person among the living nor a living person among the dead, but in an orderly sequence — as is said, ״and behold the plague had begun (החל) among the people,״ as is written (Deuteronomy 16:9): ״מהחל חרמש בקמה״ — from the beginning of the sickle in the standing grain; just as the reaper reaps in an orderly sequence — end of quotation."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:33:34.759314306Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}